  "title": "Aut(GF(pⁿ)) is Cyclic of Order n, Generated by Frobenius",
  "phase": "COMPLETED",
  "status": "graduated",
  "tier": "C",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\operatorname{Aut}\\bigl(\\mathrm{GF}(p^n)\\bigr) \\;\\cong\\; \\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}, \\qquad \\text{generated by } \\varphi : x \\mapsto x^{p}.",
    "plain": "The parent entry establishes that any two finite fields of the same cardinality are isomorphic, but the isomorphism is not canonical. This question quantifies that non-canonicity: the symmetries of $\\mathrm{GF}(p^n)$ over its prime subfield $\\mathrm{GF}(p)$ form a cyclic group of order exactly $n$, and the single generator is the Frobenius map $x \\mapsto x^p$ (raising to the $p$-th power). So there are precisely $n$ ways to identify two fields of order $p^n$, all obtained from one by composing with powers of Frobenius.",
